<article class="env-lemma" id="06G4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06G4">Lemma <span data-tag="06G4">67.28.7</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $Y$ is locally Noetherian and $f$ locally of finite type then $f$ is locally of finite presentation. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $Y$ is locally Noetherian and $f$ of finite type and quasi-separated then $f$ is of finite presentation. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume $f : X \to Y$ locally of finite type and $Y$ locally Noetherian. This means there exists a diagram as in Lemma <a href="/tag/03MJ" data-tag="03MJ">67.22.1</a> with $h$ locally of finite type and surjective vertical arrow $a$. By Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01TX" data-tag="01TX">29.22.9</a> $h$ is locally of finite presentation. Hence $X \to Y$ is locally of finite presentation by definition. This proves (1). If $f$ is of finite type and quasi-separated then it is also quasi-compact and quasi-separated and (2) follows immediately. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>